+{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Du2mj9zZXVEBDCRZwjK9B6","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Beam Splitter\n2\t\n3\tBuild a beam propagation simulator for a 2D grid laboratory.\n4\t\n5\t## Grid Format\n6\t\n7\tThe grid is a 2D array of single-character strings:\n8\t\n9\t- `'S'` — beam source (always on the top row). Emits a beam straight down.\n10\t- `'^'` — splitter. When a beam arrives, it splits into two diagonal beams.\n11\t- `'.'` — empty cell. Beams pass through.\n12\t\n13\tBeams that leave the grid boundaries are absorbed (they stop).\n14\t\n15\t## Beam Movement Rules\n16\t\n17\t1. A beam from the source `'S'` moves **straight down** (row+1, same column) each step.\n18\t2. When a beam hits a splitter `'^'`, it splits into **two beams**:\n19\t   - One goes **down-left** (row+1, col-1)\n20\t   - One goes **down-right** (row+1, col+1)\n21\t   The original beam is consumed by the splitter (it does not continue straight).\n22\t3. An unsplit beam (not at a splitter) continues in its current direction:\n23\t   - A downward beam continues downward (row+1, same col)\n24\t   - A down-left beam continues down-left (row+1, col-1)\n25\t   - A down-right beam continues down-right (row+1, col+1)\n26\t4. A beam is **absorbed** when it moves outside the grid boundaries (row < 0, row >= rows, col < 0, col >= cols).\n27\t5. Multiple beams can occupy the same cell simultaneously.\n28\t6. Splitters always split regardless of the beam's incoming direction.\n29\t\n30\t## API\n31\t\n32\tExport two functions from `src/index.ts`:\n33\t\n34\t### `countBeamCells(grid: string[][]): number`\n35\t\n36\tCount the number of distinct cells visited by any beam. A cell counts once even if multiple beams pass through it. The source cell `'S'` counts as visited. Splitter cells hit by beams count as visited.\n37\t\n38\t### `countBeamPaths(grid: string[][]): bigint`\n39\t\n40\tCount the total number of distinct source-to-exit paths through the grid. Each time a beam splits at `'^'`, both branches are separate paths. A path ends when the beam exits the grid (moves beyond any edge).\n41\t\n42\tWith K splitters along a beam's path, there are potentially 2^K paths. The result can be astronomically large, so return a `bigint`.\n43\t\n44\t**Performance constraint**: Must handle a 200x200 grid with up to 50 splitters and return results in under 5 seconds. Enumerating individual paths is not feasible for large inputs.\n45\t\n46\t## Example\n47\t\n48\t```\n49\tGrid (4 rows x 5 cols):\n50\t. S . . .\n51\t. . . . .\n52\t. ^ . . .\n53\t. . . . .\n54\t```\n55\t\n56\t- The beam starts at (0,1) where `'S'` is.\n57\t- It moves down: (1,1), (2,1) — hits splitter.\n58\t- Splits into down-left (3,0) and down-right (3,2).\n59\t- Both beams exit the grid after row 3.\n60\t- Visited cells: (0,1), (1,1), (2,1), (3,0), (3,2) → `countBeamCells` returns **5**.\n61\t- Paths: 2 distinct paths (one ending at (3,0), one at (3,2)) → `countBeamPaths` returns **2n**.\n62\t\n63\t```\n64\tGrid with two splitters:\n65\t. S . . .\n66\t. ^ . . .\n67\t^ . ^ . .\n68\t. . . . .\n69\t```\n70\t\n71\t- Beam starts at (0,1), moves to (1,1) — hits splitter.\n72\t- Splits: down-left to (2,0) hits another splitter, down-right to (2,2) hits another splitter.\n73\t- (2,0) splitter: sends beams to (3,-1) absorbed and (3,1).\n74\t- (2,2) splitter: sends beams to (3,1) and (3,3).\n75\t- `countBeamCells`: (0,1), (1,1), (2,0), (2,2), (3,1), (3,3) → **6** (note: two beams land on (3,1) but it counts once).\n76\t- `countBeamPaths`: 4 paths total, but one is absorbed → **3n** paths that exit through the bottom or sides at valid cells... Actually, all 4 paths exit (absorbed = exits grid): paths to (3,-1), (3,1), (3,1), (3,3) → **4n**.\n77\t\n78\t## Commands\n79\t\n80\t```bash\n81\tnpm test        # run tests\n82\tnpm run build   # compile TypeScript\n83\tnpm run lint    # lint source\n84\t```\n85\t\n86\t## File Structure\n87\t\n88\tPlace your implementation in `src/`. The main entry point should be `src/index.ts`.\n89\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"87665ec8-de6a-4682-b90a-37c5bd45c2e2","uuid":"e8b4c9a6-9b13-4b5f-bcf3-27d3bc5d158e","timestamp":"2026-07-01T05:29:52.814Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/TASK.md","content":"# Beam Splitter\n\nBuild a beam propagation simulator for a 2D grid laboratory.\n\n## Grid Format\n\nThe grid is a 2D array of single-character strings:\n\n- `'S'` — beam source (always on the top row). Emits a beam straight down.\n- `'^'` — splitter. When a beam arrives, it splits into two diagonal beams.\n- `'.'` — empty cell. Beams pass through.\n\nBeams that leave the grid boundaries are absorbed (they stop).\n\n## Beam Movement Rules\n\n1. A beam from the source `'S'` moves **straight down** (row+1, same column) each step.\n2. When a beam hits a splitter `'^'`, it splits into **two beams**:\n   - One goes **down-left** (row+1, col-1)\n   - One goes **down-right** (row+1, col+1)\n   The original beam is consumed by the splitter (it does not continue straight).\n3. An unsplit beam (not at a splitter) continues in its current direction:\n   - A downward beam continues downward (row+1, same col)\n   - A down-left beam continues down-left (row+1, col-1)\n   - A down-right beam continues down-right (row+1, col+1)\n4. A beam is **absorbed** when it moves outside the grid boundaries (row < 0, row >= rows, col < 0, col >= cols).\n5. Multiple beams can occupy the same cell simultaneously.\n6. Splitters always split regardless of the beam's incoming direction.\n\n## API\n\nExport two functions from `src/index.ts`:\n\n### `countBeamCells(grid: string[][]): number`\n\nCount the number of distinct cells visited by any beam. A cell counts once even if multiple beams pass through it. The source cell `'S'` counts as visited. Splitter cells hit by beams count as visited.\n\n### `countBeamPaths(grid: string[][]): bigint`\n\nCount the total number of distinct source-to-exit paths through the grid. Each time a beam splits at `'^'`, both branches are separate paths. A path ends when the beam exits the grid (moves beyond any edge).\n\nWith K splitters along a beam's path, there are potentially 2^K paths. The result can be astronomically large, so return a `bigint`.\n\n**Performance constraint**: Must handle a 200x200 grid with up to 50 splitters and return results in under 5 seconds. Enumerating individual paths is not feasible for large inputs.\n\n## Example\n\n```\nGrid (4 rows x 5 cols):\n. S . . .\n. . . . .\n. ^ . . .\n. . . . .\n```\n\n- The beam starts at (0,1) where `'S'` is.\n- It moves down: (1,1), (2,1) — hits splitter.\n- Splits into down-left (3,0) and down-right (3,2).\n- Both beams exit the grid after row 3.\n- Visited cells: (0,1), (1,1), (2,1), (3,0), (3,2) → `countBeamCells` returns **5**.\n- Paths: 2 distinct paths (one ending at (3,0), one at (3,2)) → `countBeamPaths` returns **2n**.\n\n```\nGrid with two splitters:\n. S . . .\n. ^ . . .\n^ . ^ . .\n. . . . .\n```\n\n- Beam starts at (0,1), moves to (1,1) — hits splitter.\n- Splits: down-left to (2,0) hits another splitter, down-right to (2,2) hits another splitter.\n- (2,0) splitter: sends beams to (3,-1) absorbed and (3,1).\n- (2,2) splitter: sends beams to (3,1) and (3,3).\n- `countBeamCells`: (0,1), (1,1), (2,0), (2,2), (3,1), (3,3) → **6** (note: two beams land on (3,1) but it counts once).\n- `countBeamPaths`: 4 paths total, but one is absorbed → **3n** paths that exit through the bottom or sides at valid cells... Actually, all 4 paths exit (absorbed = exits grid): paths to (3,-1), (3,1), (3,1), (3,3) → **4n**.\n\n## Commands\n\n```bash\nnpm test        # run tests\nnpm run build   # compile TypeScript\nnpm run lint    # lint source\n```\n\n## File Structure\n\nPlace your implementation in `src/`. The main entry point should be `src/index.ts`.\n","numLines":89,"startLine":1,"totalLines":89}}}
